setting her sights on Sachi corporal  Amanda Lightfoot one of two British  soldiers taking time out from the Army  to focus on an Olympic target Amanda and  Lance Bombardier lead Jackson of both  serving soldiers compete under the  British flag in the biathlon in Russia  their sport is a demanding combination  of cross-country skiing and rifle  shooting and the Army has a long  tradition of sending athletes to the  Olympics Amanda is the latest product  completing a journey that began in the  sand of Iraq I was out there and there  was a officer looking for fit females to  join the regimental ski team for the agc  Corps and I got picked up from there  basically originally I thought I was  gonna be going downhill skiing I'd never  actually been on snow in my life let  alone stand on cross-country skis so  that was a crazy journey to come from  there and I remember my first days on  snow I could hardly even stand up you  know and then you're getting tall the  ski up a hill with a rifle attached to  your back the British team made their  final preparations in Ridd now in the  Italian Tyrol the scenery is idyllic the  competition is fierce this is the key to  the biathlon the shooting the skiers  arrive here with their heart's pounding  upwards of 175 beats a minute they've  got to shoot five targets 50 metres away  no more than two inches across be in and  out less than 30 seconds  you have to be up early to get your mat  down on the range before the Germans  they're one of many teams with far  larger resources than the British thanks  to the sports huge popularity in Europe  biathlon World Cup events draw crowds of  up to 40,000 with millions more watching  on TV the British athletes and their  two-man support team could only compete  because the army allows them to train  full-time the Ministry of Defence is to  cut direct funding to the sport after  Saatchi and while a new sponsor will  help fill the gap the sport faces a  challenging future I think there's a lot  of respect from the other nations but  norther actually we are lacking in  resources lacking in participation  numbers and also the fact that we don't  start doing biathlon until 1012 years  after you know the rest of the world  kind of start doing battles but with a  wealth of military experience they've  served in Afghanistan Iraq Bosnia and  Northern Ireland between them they're  now chasing medals of a different kind  Paul Kelso Sky News
 

